@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import {
  task id, brief, child run, failure kind, domain event, child port, session usage,
    launch spec, attach command, child session, session export, child driver,
    child working directory,
    starter tools, prerequisites check, model listing, model check,
    follow-up message, steering message, turn steering, session resume, turn,
    unread steering message,
}
@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
  agent definition, skill folder, project root, timeout, thinking level,
  tool list, general agent, fallback agent, child host options,
}
@design/task-store.sigil from TaskStore import {
  task record, sessions folder, driver files,
}
@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import { driver registry, entry point, child host }

component PiDriver {
  goal {
    Start an isolated pi child that sees only the declared skills, run each
    turn of a task to settlement on its child session, deliver each
    steering message into the turn that is running, and stop it cleanly in
    every exit case.
  }

  state {
    PiDriver fills the launch spec of each child run with the argv of pi
    without its prompt, its child working directory, the names of its environment
    variables, the pi version, its skill folder paths and the thinking level of
    the agent definition.

    A *skill overlay* is the folder skills/ inside the folder of the
    driver files
    that the child port hands over for the task. It is owned by PiDriver and is
    not
    part of the task record, and it holds a copy of each declared skill folder
    side
    by side, so that relative paths between sibling skills keep working. The
    skill overlay is among the driver files of the task.

    The child session of a task is the pi conversation of that task, kept as a
    pi
    session file, and every turn of the task adds to it.
  }

  logic {
    Before spawning, check that every skill folder of the agent definition
    exists,
    and stop with the failure kind skill_mismatch if one is missing. Then check
    the child host options for pi, and stop with the failure kind error and the
    message of the check if it fails. Copy each
    declared skill folder into the skill overlay, whose paths the launch spec
    uses.
    Hand the launch spec to the caller in the run started domain event before
    spawning, which ends that branch. Spawn pi from the launch spec as the
    leader
    of its own process group, with the project root as child working directory as
    the
    constraints require. Send get_state, whose reply means pi is ready, and
    then
    get_commands, whose reply goes to skill verification. Perform
    *skill verification* by comparing the skills in the get_commands reply with
    the
    declared skills. On a difference, stop with the failure kind skill_mismatch
    naming the extra or missing skill, send no prompt and kill the process
    group.

    Otherwise, once skill verification has passed as the constraints require,
    send the prompt, which is the brief, or the follow-up message for a
    session resume, report the prompt sent domain event to the caller, and read
    events until agent_settled. Map each
    *pi event* to a domain event and hand it to the caller, which ends that
    branch.
    After agent_settled, read the last assistant text and the session usage,
    close
    stdin and wait for the child run to exit, and the exit lets the flow
    return.
    Return the last assistant text, the session usage, a status taken from the
    stop
    reason of the last assistant message and, for a failed run, its
    failure kind,
    which ends the flow. A stop reason of stop means completed, length means
    truncated, error means failed with the failure kind error and a short
    bounded
    message from pi, and aborted means cancelled with no failure kind. A
    child run
    that exits cleanly with no assistant message is failed with the
    failure kind
    error and the message no assistant message. Fill the session usage from
    what pi
    reports, and mark each field that pi does not report as unavailable.

    On session resume, start pi from the same flag set and the same
    sessions folder, which reopens the child session of the task because its
    id is the task id, perform skill verification as for any child run, and
    send the follow-up message as the prompt, after which the child run goes on
    as above, which ends that flow. Before it starts pi, check that the session
    file of the child session exists, and when it does not, start no pi and report
    that the child session is gone, which ends that flow. Before it sends the
    prompt, read get_session_stats and keep it as the usage baseline, and at settle
    report the session usage as the difference from that baseline, because pi
    reports tokens and cost across the whole session, and the context use at settle
    as pi reports it.

    On turn steering, send the steering message to pi with the steer command
    while the child run is live. When pi accepts it before agent_settled,
    restart the timeout of the child run and report that it was delivered,
    which ends that flow. When the child run has already reached
    agent_settled, send nothing and report that it had already settled, which
    ends that flow. When a stop is pending, send nothing and report that the
    child run is stopping, which ends that flow. Read the queue_update events of pi
    while the child run is live, and at agent_settled report each steering message
    that pi still holds as queued as an unread steering message, because pi makes
    its last queue check before it emits agent_settled, which ends that flow.
  }

  constraints {
    The driver must start a child run with exactly this flag set in its
    launch spec, with the project root as child working directory and with
    PI_OFFLINE=1
    and PI_TELEMETRY=0 in its environment. It must not add --session, because
    pi
    exits when that is combined with --session-id:
    ```
    pi --mode rpc --offline -na -ne -nc -np --no-themes -ns
       [-e <extension> ...]
       --skill <overlay>/<name> ... --model <provider/id> --thinking <level>
       --tools <tools> --session-dir <sessions folder>
       --session-id <task id> --name subzero:<agent>:<task id>
    ```

    PiDriver owns the meaning of the child host options for pi: a list under
    the key extensions of paths to the files of a *pi extension*, each path
    absolute or starting with ~/, which the driver expands from the home folder
    and de-duplicates, keeping the first. The driver adds one -e flag with its
    path for each pi extension, in the order given, and no -e flag when the
    caller gives none. The attach command carries the same -e flags. Before it
    starts pi for a child run, a model listing or a model check, the driver must
    check the child host options for pi, and when they hold a key other than
    extensions, the list is malformed or one pi extension names no file it must
    fail without starting pi, with a one-line message that names the problem
    and the path, so that a misspelled key is not ignored, because pi gives no
    warning of a missing path and the models of that pi extension would then
    show only as unknown. A map that holds nothing counts as no pi extension. For a
    child run it makes that check right after the check of the
    skill folders and before it hands over the launch spec.

    A pi extension is code of the user, which the user named in the child host
    options, and PiDriver treats it as trusted. Against a pi extension the driver
    guarantees only two things, and every other constraint of PiDriver still
    holds: the tools of the child run are those of its tool list, because pi
    1.5.2 applies that tool list to the tools that a pi extension adds, which
    PiDriver verifies with its child flags on that version, and the skills are
    exactly the declared skills. A pi extension may still change the prompt or run
    code of
    its own, so a read-only tool list of the fallback agent limits its tools
    and is no sandbox against a pi extension.

    PiDriver receives the project root, the sessions folder, the folder of
    its driver files and the child host options through the child port each
    time it starts a child run,
    resolves
    none of its own, and calls neither TaskStore nor the delegation service.
    PiDriver must not write the task record, and hands the
    launch spec to the caller so that TaskCore writes it.

    A child run must see exactly the declared skills and no user or project
    extensions other than the pi extensions in its child host options, and no
    context files, prompt templates or other skills. It must run
    under
    the user's own pi login, and no credentials may be copied.

    A missing skill folder, or any difference found by skill verification, must
    fail the call as skill_mismatch before the brief is sent.

    The driver must use its own *pi RPC client* for pi 1.5.2, the version the
    plan
    targets, and must verify its child flags on that version before use, and
    not
    the
    client that
    pi exports. It must split stdout on LF only, strip a trailing CR, decode
    UTF-8
    from a byte buffer, correlate each response to its command by id, and never
    use
    Node readline. A reply to a command of a child run must not be subject to
    the
    60-second wait of the exported client.

    The status of a child run that the driver ends by timeout, cancel, crash or
    unknown model must come from that termination and not from the stop reason
    of
    its last assistant message, and so must its failure kind when it is failed.
    When a cancel and a timeout coincide, the first termination wins and is
    recorded once, and a cancel that arrives after the child run has settled
    changes nothing.

    A child run settles only at agent_settled, never at agent_end.

    A pi event of an unrecognized type must be logged and ignored, and the
    child run must continue.

    Agent message deltas must be buffered into one domain event and not
    forwarded
    per token.

    The timeout of the agent definition must start when the driver spawns the
    child run and cover skill verification and the turn, it must start again
    each time the driver delivers a steering message into that child run, and
    it must not count the time that the child run waited Pending for its
    caller.

    A steering message counts as delivered when pi accepts the steer command
    before agent_settled with no stop pending, whether pi answers queued or
    handled, because the responses and the events of pi arrive in order on
    one stdout. pi does not promise that an accepted message stays queued,
    so the driver must report as unread each steering message that is still
    queued at agent_settled, and must answer a steering message that arrives
    while a stop is pending as stopping and send nothing. The prompt sent
    domain event must be reported only once pi has accepted the prompt
    command, and never for a child run that failed skill verification,
    because TaskCore takes from it that the task has a child session. The
    session file, whose path the driver sets before it sends the prompt,
    proves no child session by itself.

    A steering message must be sent with the steer command of pi and never
    with follow_up, so that it joins the running turn and that turn still
    settles once, at agent_settled. The driver must verify on pi 1.5.2, as for
    its child flags, that a steering message that pi accepts during the last
    model call of a turn is read before agent_settled, and that starting pi
    with the same --session-id and sessions folder reopens the existing
    child session.

    When a child run exceeds its timeout, exits non-zero in mid-turn or names
    an
    unknown model, it must end with the failure kind with value timeout, crashed or
    error.
    A
    crashed child run records the stderr tail as a domain event, and an unknown
    model error names the model. A crashed child run is a process failure,
    which is
    a non-zero exit in mid-turn or a lost pipe, and an error is a run-level
    error
    that pi reports through its stop reason, an unknown model, or a precondition
    that the driver found missing before it started pi, such as a pi extension
    that names no file.

    The driver must not remove the skill overlay of a task while its
    task record
    exists, because pi ignores a missing --skill path without an error and an
    attached child session would then have no skills.

    Cancel and timeout of a child run must send clear_queue, then abort and
    wait up
    to five seconds, then close stdin, then send SIGTERM and then SIGKILL to
    the
    process group. No process of the group may remain, including a grandchild.

    The driver must not unref a child run or let it outlive its process: the
    child run starts as the leader of its own process group only so that the
    whole
    group
    can be killed, and it keeps the pipes of its stdio. When the process that
    runs
    the driver dies, stdin of the child run is closed and pi must exit, so no
    process of a child run may outlive Esc, a timeout or the end of the parent
    session.

    When ~/.pi/agent/SYSTEM.md exists, the driver must record a warning as a
    domain event and still start the child run, because -nc does not cover it.

    The id of the child session must be the task id, and the sessions folder
    must
    be inside the project root and not the user's own pi sessions.
  }

  cases {
    When skill verification finds exactly the declared skills, the brief is
    sent
    and the child run settles with the last assistant text as its summary.

    When skill verification finds one extra skill, the child run fails as
    skill_mismatch naming it, no prompt is sent and the process is killed.

    A steering message sent while pi runs a tool call is accepted, the timeout
    of the child run starts again, and the child run still settles once with
    the last assistant text as its summary. A steering message that arrives
    after agent_settled is not sent, and the driver reports that the child run
    had already settled.

    A steering message that pi accepts after its last queue check, so that
    the child run settles without reading it, is reported as unread, and one
    that arrives while a stop is pending is answered stopping. A session
    resume whose session file is gone starts no pi and reports that the
    child session is gone, and the session usage of a resumed turn is the
    session stats at settle less those read before its prompt.

    A session resume of a task whose first child run settled starts pi with
    the same flag set, and the child reads the follow-up message as the next
    message of the same child session.

    When pi emits agent_end, then more events, then agent_settled, the
    child run
    settles only at agent_settled.

    A message of a child run that contains U+2028 is delivered as one record,
    and a
    multi-byte UTF-8 character split across two stdout chunks decodes
    correctly.
  }

  interface {
    PiDriver provides the child port: for an agent definition, a brief and a
    task id, start a child run, perform skill verification, run its prompt to
    settlement, and return the last assistant text, the status, the
    failure kind of
    a failed run and the session usage. Allow a live child run to be cancelled.

    Offer turn steering, which delivers a steering message into a live
    child run or reports that it had already settled, and session resume, which
    starts a child run on the child session of a task with a follow-up message
    as its prompt. Offer both as optional offerings, and make whether it steers
    known before any child run exists, so that a caller can answer queued or not
    delivered with no child run.
    Keep the skill overlay of each task for as long as its task record exists.

    Hand each domain event of a live child run to the caller as it happens,
    with
    the tool name and its target path in the domain event of each tool
    activity.

    Build the attach command from the launch spec of a task, without --mode rpc
    and without --session, so that the child session can be reopened in the pi
    terminal interface.

    Hand the launch spec of every child run to the caller in the run started
    domain event, so that the task record keeps it.

    Export the child session of a task as HTML with pi --export, as the
    session export of the child driver for the child host pi.

    Offer a prerequisites check that runs the configured pi command with
    --version, passes when it exits zero, and otherwise returns a message that
    names pi, says whether it was not found or did not run, and says to install
    pi and run subzero init again.

    Offer a model listing that asks pi for get_available_models with the
    configured pi command, the environment, the isolation flags of a
    child run and the pi extensions in the child host options that the caller
    gives, and no saved
    session, so that a model that a child run cannot reach is not listed and a
    model that a pi extension provides is. Give each model the thinking levels
    it accepts: only off for a model that does not reason, and otherwise each level
    from off to
    high that its thinking level map does not mark null, and xhigh or max only
    when the map gives them a value. Fail with a one-line reason when pi does
    not answer within 30 seconds, and with a reason that says to set up a pi
    provider when pi lists no model.

    Offer a model check that starts pi with the same command, environment,
    flags and child host options, the model, the thinking level, no tools and no
    saved session, sends one fixed prompt and reports that the model answered when
    the turn settles
    without an error. Report unknown_model when pi does not know the model,
    timeout when no answer comes within 120 seconds, cancelled when the caller
    aborts it, and failed with the reason from pi otherwise, or with the reason
    of the check of the child host options for pi when that check fails before
    pi starts, and end the pi process in every case.

    Register with the driver registry of Kernel as the child driver for the
    child host pi
    when an entry point of a process registers it, and offer turn steering,
    session resume, the attach command,
    the session export, the starter tools, the prerequisites check, the
    model listing and the model check through it.
    The starter tools are the tool list read, bash, edit, write, grep, find and
    ls for a general agent, and the tool list read, grep, find and ls for a
    fallback agent.
  }

  decisions {
    The driver spawns the user's installed pi and does not embed the pi SDK, so
    that it uses the same login, models and version, can kill by process group,
    and
    a crash in the child cannot take down the server. A failure kind is still
    decided by the driver from what pi did.
  }
}
